!id: MFZGG2uCEf
!front: State the completeness (least upper bound) axiom of \( \mathbb{R} \).
!back:
If \( A \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) is:

- nonempty, and
- bounded above,

then \( A \) has a least upper bound \( \sup A \in \mathbb{R} \).
!extra: This is taken as an axiom of \( \mathbb{R} \), not derived from the field and order axioms — it is exactly the property that distinguishes \( \mathbb{R} \) from \( \mathbb{Q} \).
~~~

!id: 828qKjoRsk
!front: Let \( A = \{x \in \mathbb{Q} : x^2 < 2\} \). Is \( A \) bounded above in \( \mathbb{Q} \)? Does it have a least upper bound in \( \mathbb{Q} \)?
!back:
\( A \) is bounded above in \( \mathbb{Q} \) — e.g. \( 2 \) is a rational upper bound.

\( A \) has no least upper bound in \( \mathbb{Q} \): the natural candidate is \( \sqrt{2} \), which is irrational, and for any rational upper bound \( q > \sqrt{2} \), a rational \( q' \) with \( \sqrt{2} < q' < q \) can always be found, so \( q \) is never the smallest rational upper bound.
!extra:
This is why completeness must be added as an axiom: \( \mathbb{Q} \) satisfies the field and order axioms of \( \mathbb{R} \) but fails completeness.

![The rationals below sqrt(2) have no least upper bound in Q](img/rational-gap.svg)
~~~
